Day 68 - Min Stack

Problem Statement:
Design a stack that supports push, pop, top, and retrieving the minimum element in constant time.
Each operation must have O(1) time complexity.

Code:
class MinStack {
public:
    stack<pair<int, int>> s;

    MinStack() {
        
    }
    
    void push(int val) {
        if(s.empty()) {
            s.push({val, val});
        }
        else {
            int minVal = min(val, s.top().second);
            s.push({val, minVal});
        }
    }
    
    void pop() {
        s.pop();
    }
    
    int top() {
        return s.top().first;
    }
    
    int getMin() {
        return s.top().second;
    }
};





Time Complexity:
O(1) for push, pop, top, and getMin.

Space Complexity:
O(n)

The stack stores n elements, and each element contains a pair of values.

Approach:
This solution uses a stack of pairs.
Each pair stores:
{value, minimum_so_far}

For every element, we keep track of the minimum value from the bottom of the stack up to that element.
When pushing a new value:
If the stack is empty, both values are val.
Otherwise, compare val with the current minimum s.top().second.
Store the smaller value as the new minimum.
Push {val, minVal} into the stack.

For pop():
Simply remove the top pair.

For top():
Return s.top().first, which contains the actual value.

For getMin():
Return s.top().second, which contains the minimum value so far.
